var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Writing","page":"Writing","text":"XzWriter is a BufferIO AbstractBufWriter that compresses what is written to it. Like the readers, it wraps an AbstractBufWriter or an IO, and closing it finishes the .xz data and closes the sink. flush ends the current Block, so that everything written so far can be decoded.\n\njulia> XzWriter(open(\"hello.txt.xz\", \"w\")) do writer\n           write(writer, \"Hello, world!\")\n       end\n13\n\nIf the function passed to XzWriter throws, the .xz data is left unfinished, so that it isn't mistaken for complete data.","title":"Writing"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Options","page":"Writing","text":"Keyword Default Meaning\nlevel 6 Compression preset, from 0 (fastest) to 9 (smallest)\ncheck :crc64 Integrity check: :none, :crc32, :crc64 or :sha256\nthreads 0 liblzma threads, with 0 for one per CPU\nblocksize three times the dictionary size, at least 1 MiB Uncompressed bytes per Block, with 0 for one Block\n\nSmaller Blocks make random access with XzSeekableReader faster, and compress slightly worse. With more than one thread, XzWriter writes each Block once it is complete, recording its size. With one thread, it writes output as input arrives.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Whole-buffers","page":"Writing","text":"compress_xz and decompress_xz take the same keywords as XzWriter and XzReader.\n\njulia> compressed = compress_xz(\"Hello, world!\"; level=9);\n\njulia> String(decompress_xz(compressed))\n\"Hello, world!\"","title":"Whole buffers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Writing","page":"API","text":"","title":"Writing"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reading","page":"API","text":"","title":"Reading"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Errors","page":"API","text":"","title":"Errors"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#XzLib","page":"API","text":"xz compression and decompression on BufferIO.\n\nXzWriter compresses what is written to it.\nXzReader decompresses a stream of .xz data.\nXzSeekableReader decompresses seekable .xz data with random access.\ncompress_xz and decompress_xz work on whole buffers.\nXzError reports liblzma failures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"XzLib"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#XzLib.XzWriter","page":"API","text":"XzWriter(sink::Union{IO, AbstractBufWriter}; level=6, check=:crc64, threads=0, blocksize=nothing, bufsize=65536)\nXzWriter(f, sink; kwargs...)\n\nCreate an AbstractBufWriter that writes the .xz compression of what is written to it to sink.\n\nflush ends the current Block, so that everything written so far can be decoded. close finishes the .xz data and closes sink. The second form calls f on the writer then closes it, unless f throws, in which case the .xz data are left unfinished so that they don't pass for complete.\n\nArguments\n\nlevel: compression preset, from 0 (fastest) to 9 (smallest)\ncheck: integrity check, one of :none, :crc32, :crc64, or :sha256\nthreads: number of liblzma threads, or 0 for one per available CPU like xz -T0; either way reduced as needed to keep memory usage within a quarter of RAM\nblocksize: uncompressed bytes per Block, by default xz's: three times the dictionary size, and at least 1 MiB (24 MiB at level 6). Smaller Blocks compress slightly worse but make seeking in XzSeekableReader cheaper. 0 writes a single Block, on one thread.\nbufsize: size of the input buffer as bytes\n\nWith one thread, output is written as input arrives. With more, each Block is written once it is complete, recording its sizes, which lets liblzma decode it on its own thread.\n\nThrows ArgumentError for invalid arguments, and XzError if liblzma fails.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using XzLib\n\njulia> path = tempname();\n\njulia> XzWriter(open(path, \"w\"); level=9) do writer\n           write(writer, \"Hello, world!\")\n       end\n13\n\njulia> String(decompress_xz(read(path)))\n\"Hello, world!\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"XzLib.XzWriter"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#XzLib.compress_xz","page":"API","text":"compress_xz(data; kwargs...)::Vector{UInt8}\n\nCompress data, anything write accepts, to .xz. The keyword arguments are those of XzWriter.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using XzLib\n\njulia> compressed = compress_xz(\"Hello, world!\");\n\njulia> String(decompress_xz(compressed))\n\"Hello, world!\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"XzLib.compress_xz"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#XzLib.XzReader","page":"API","text":"XzReader(source::Union{IO, AbstractBufReader}; threads=0, memlimit=18446744073709551615, bufsize=65536)\nXzReader(f, source; kwargs...)\n\nCreate an AbstractBufReader that decompresses the .xz data read from source, which may be several concatenated .xz streams. The second form calls f on the reader then closes it.\n\nFor random access, see XzSeekableReader.\n\nArguments\n\nthreads: number of liblzma decoding threads, or 0 for one per available CPU. Blocks decode in parallel only when they record their sizes (see XzWriter).\nmemlimit: memory usage limit as bytes\nbufsize: size of the output buffer as bytes\n\nThrows ArgumentError for invalid arguments, and XzError if the data are corrupt, after which reads throw the same error.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using XzLib\n\njulia> reader = XzReader(IOBuffer(compress_xz(\"Hello, world!\")));\n\njulia> read(reader, String)\n\"Hello, world!\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"XzLib.XzReader"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#XzLib.XzSeekableReader","page":"API","text":"XzSeekableReader(source::Union{IO, AbstractBufReader}; threads=Threads.nthreads(), memlimit=18446744073709551615, bufsize=65536)\nXzSeekableReader(f, source; kwargs...)\n\nCreate a seekable AbstractBufReader that decompresses the .xz data source is positioned at the start of, which must extend to the end of source. The second form calls f on the reader then closes it.\n\nseek, skip, position, and filesize all work in uncompressed offsets. Seeking itself is cheap: the next read decodes from the start of the Block holding its first byte, so its cost is bounded by the file's Block size (see blocksize in XzWriter), and forward seeks within the current Block decode on rather than restart. Once reading proves sequential, up to threads of the following Blocks are decoded ahead on worker tasks, in parallel when Julia has several threads.\n\nWrap the reader in BufferIO.IOReader to pass it where a Base.IO is expected.\n\nArguments\n\nsource: seekable IO holding .xz data\nthreads: maximum number of Blocks to decode ahead, with 1 decoding only on the reading task\nmemlimit: memory usage limit per Block decoder as bytes\nbufsize: size of the output buffer as bytes\n\nThrows XzError if the .xz Index cannot be read, and while reading if the data are corrupt, after which reads throw until the next seek.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using XzLib\n\njulia> data = repeat(b\"0123456789\", 100_000);\n\njulia> reader = XzSeekableReader(IOBuffer(compress_xz(data; blocksize=100_000)));\n\njulia> seek(reader, 654_321);\n\njulia> String(read(reader, 4))\n\"1234\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"XzLib.XzSeekableReader"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#XzLib.decompress_xz","page":"API","text":"decompress_xz(data; kwargs...)::Vector{UInt8}\n\nDecompress the .xz data, a byte vector or string. The keyword arguments are those of XzReader.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"XzLib.decompress_xz"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#XzLib.XzError","page":"API","text":"XzError <: Exception\n\nFailure to encode or decode .xz data while attempting operation, with the liblzma status code that caused it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"XzLib.XzError"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Reading","page":"Reading","text":"XzLib has two readers, both BufferIO readers (AbstractBufReader):\n\nXzReader decompresses .xz data as it is read, from any source, including pipes and sockets. With more than one thread, it decodes Blocks in parallel when they record their sizes.\nXzSeekableReader reads the Index of complete .xz data from a seekable source, and then supports seek, skip, position and filesize in uncompressed offsets. It decodes Blocks ahead on Julia's threads, whether or not they record their sizes.\n\nFor files, use XzSeekableReader with Julia started with several threads (-t). For streams, or with a single Julia thread, use XzReader.\n\nBoth wrap an AbstractBufReader, or wrap an IO in a BufReader first. Closing a reader closes its source, and passing a function as the first argument calls it on the reader and then closes it.\n\njulia> reader = XzReader(IOBuffer(compress_xz(\"Hello, world!\")));\n\njulia> String(read(reader))\n\"Hello, world!\"","title":"Reading"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Random-access","page":"Reading","text":"A read from XzSeekableReader decodes from the start of the Block containing its first byte, so files written with smaller Blocks seek faster.\n\njulia> data = repeat(b\"0123456789\", 100_000);\n\njulia> reader = XzSeekableReader(IOBuffer(compress_xz(data; blocksize=100_000)));\n\njulia> seek(reader, 654_321);\n\njulia> String(read(reader, 4))\n\"1234\"","title":"Random access"},{"category":"section","location":"reading/#Errors","page":"Reading","text":"Corrupt or truncated data throws an XzError. An XzReader throws the same error on every later read, while an XzSeekableReader can be recovered by seeking.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"#XzLib.jl","page":"Introduction","text":"Read and write .xz data with BufferIO.jl, including random access into files split into Blocks.\n\nusing XzLib\n\n# Compress a file\nXzWriter(open(\"data.xz\", \"w\")) do writer\n    write(writer, read(\"data\"))\nend\n\n# Decompress it\ndata = XzReader(read, open(\"data.xz\"))\n\n# Read 100 bytes from the middle\nXzSeekableReader(open(\"data.xz\")) do reader\n    seek(reader, 5_000_000)\n    read(reader, 100)\nend","title":"XzLib.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Comparison-with-CodecXz","page":"Introduction","text":"CodecXz.jl provides .xz codecs for TranscodingStreams.jl, and is the one to use with other TranscodingStreams packages. XzLib started as a rewrite of CodecXz. It uses BufferIO's interface instead, and adds random access and parallel decoding of any multi-Block file.","title":"Comparison with CodecXz"}]
}
